WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:03.320
The annual flu shot has been 
a consistent staple

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:06.056
in many of our lives since the 1940s.

00:00:06.056 --> 00:00:09.190
And for the past 80 years, the way 
we research and make it

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.649
has remained mostly unchanged.

00:00:11.649 --> 00:00:14.440
Which is not really a problem.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:18.330
The flu shot is estimated to save 
thousands of lives every year

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.851
and prevent millions of cases 
in the United States alone.

00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:26.314
In other words, it’s a safe and effective
way to stop the seasonal flu strain.

00:00:26.314 --> 00:00:30.510
But what if something
different emerged?

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.230
The last four pandemics before Covid-19

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.530
were brought on by an influenza virus, 
not a coronavirus.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.302
And virologists agree that when it
comes to the next flu pandemic,

00:00:41.302 --> 00:00:44.266
it’s not if, but when.

00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:49.619
But the Covid-19 pandemic has brought us 
new advances in vaccine technology.

00:00:49.619 --> 00:00:53.177
And now we're closer than ever before 
to something new.

00:00:53.177 --> 00:00:59.070
Something like a flu shot that protects us
year after year, regardless of the strain.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:02.883
Something like a universal flu vaccine.

00:01:05.696 --> 00:01:08.189
There are a lot of different types of influenza

00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:12.990
and researchers identify each one by looking at 
two proteins on the surface of the virus:

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.039
hemagglutinin and neuraminidase.

00:01:16.039 --> 00:01:21.499
Each one has various subtypes, which can create
a ton of different combinations.

00:01:21.499 --> 00:01:23.692
And that’s just influenza A viruses.

00:01:23.692 --> 00:01:26.238
There’s also influenza B to worry about.

00:01:26.238 --> 00:01:29.470
A truly universal vaccine would have 
to cover all of these

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:31.995
plus anything that could potentially emerge.

00:01:31.995 --> 00:01:38.716
SCHULTZ-CHERRY: So what makes influenza
unique is its ability to change rapidly.

00:01:38.716 --> 00:01:41.902
That's why we have to update 
our vaccines every year,

00:01:41.902 --> 00:01:45.510
because as it goes through people, 
it changes.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.470
The trouble is, making the vaccine takes 
a lot of time every year,

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.180
so in order to have a vaccine ready 
for flu season

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.611
researchers have to rely on a bit of guesswork.

00:01:54.611 --> 00:01:56.082
FULLER: We have to almost predict

00:01:56.082 --> 00:01:59.228
“What is next year's flu virus 
going to look like?”

00:01:59.228 --> 00:02:03.170
Since the flu is a seasonal virus that 
typically appears in the colder months

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:06.190
scientists predict the most common strains
by looking at outbreaks

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.825
in other areas of the world.

00:02:07.825 --> 00:02:10.110
SCHULTZ-CHERRY: They'll make the decision
for February

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.537
for that next September's vaccine 
in the northern hemisphere.

00:02:14.537 --> 00:02:18.900
Researchers typically choose the top 4 
most common strains in one hemisphere

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.860
to make the flu vaccine 
for the other hemisphere.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.651
But since the virus is constantly changing,

00:02:24.651 --> 00:02:29.113
the predictions in February aren’t always
100% accurate by September.

00:02:29.113 --> 00:02:31.861
FULLER: The efficacy of the flu vaccine can vary

00:02:31.861 --> 00:02:36.670
from as little as 30%, up to 70%
from any one given year

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:40.810
simply because those predictions 
are not always perfect.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:42.907
They're pretty good, 
but they're not perfect.

00:02:42.907 --> 00:02:47.520
One solution would be to just make the vaccine
closer to flu season.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.663
But the way we currently make the flu vaccine
makes that impossible.

00:02:51.663 --> 00:02:57.300
SCHULTZ-CHERRY: Right now, our current practice takes 
6 to 8 months to make the vaccine.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.395
Scientists have used fertilized chicken eggs
to grow viruses for vaccines since the 40’s.

00:03:02.395 --> 00:03:06.200
In fact, we use so many eggs 
to make flu vaccines annually

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:11.655
that the US government actually has top secret
chicken farms for this specific purpose.

00:03:11.655 --> 00:03:14.540
But that’s a story for another time.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.510
The point is, when a virus is prone 
to changing rapidly,

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.280
6 to 8 months is a ton of wasted time.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.440
Newer vaccine technology like mRNA 
could dramatically shrink this timeline

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.200
because it doesn’t require growing 
large quantities of viruses.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.029
As in, we could do away with 
the chickens and the eggs.

00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:37.030
We’d likely be able to make a much more
effective flu vaccine

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:39.088
because we’d be developing shots 
closer to the time

00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:42.569
we actually discover the new strain.

00:03:42.569 --> 00:03:48.349
This is one way to potentially improve the
flu shot, but only up to a point.

00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:52.690
Because making a vaccine faster 
doesn’t make it universal.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:59.356
To do that, we have to teach our immune system
to target a different part of the virus entirely.

00:03:59.356 --> 00:04:03.234
Influenza changes fast,
but not the entire virus.

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:07.090
Just a few pieces - specifically the top part
of that one protein

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:10.590
called hemagglutinin, which is
kind of shaped like a tulip.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:12.010
SCHULTZ-CHERRY: The head of the tulip,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.240
that's where we currently
make antibodies to.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.189
And that's the area that changes
super fast,

00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:21.043
so it’ll go from a red to a white
to a yellow.

00:04:21.043 --> 00:04:24.110
In simplest terms, our annual flu shot 
teaches our body

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.629
to recognize the top of that protein: 
the "tulip head."

00:04:27.629 --> 00:04:32.095
Which is why one vaccine that teaches our
immune system to fight off a yellow tulip

00:04:32.095 --> 00:04:35.669
isn’t good for the next year 
when a red tulip shows up.

00:04:35.669 --> 00:04:37.430
For a universal vaccine to work

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:40.339
it needs to teach our immune system 
to recognize and fight off

00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:44.050
a part of the virus that doesn’t change
as much.

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.680
Which means instead of focusing on the head

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.120
that changes year after year,
from virus to virus,

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.537
many researchers have turned
their attention to the stem.

00:04:52.537 --> 00:04:56.780
FULLER: The hemagglutinin stem has been a target 
for universal flu vaccines

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.118
for quite some time.

00:04:58.118 --> 00:05:02.849
KRAMMER: The problem with our immune system, 
is that it doesn't like to actually target the stalk.

00:05:02.849 --> 00:05:05.620
So you basically have to find a way 
to convince the immune system

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:07.879
that it finds the stalk attractive.

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:09.589
There are a few ways to do that.

00:05:09.589 --> 00:05:13.449
Some researchers have tried 
removing the head all together.

00:05:13.449 --> 00:05:16.360
Others have found promise in swapping out
the head multiple times

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:19.809
so your immune system only recognizes 
the stem as a threat,

00:05:19.809 --> 00:05:23.484
and builds a stronger immune response to that.

00:05:23.484 --> 00:05:25.930
Now, there are also other parts of the virus
that don’t change as much

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.770
that researchers are looking at, 
which will likely be vital

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.788
for an effective universal flu vaccine as well.

00:05:31.788 --> 00:05:33.669
FULLER: There's always the possibility 
that down the line

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:36.050
once you generate, say, 
a universal flu vaccine

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.289
focused on the hemagglutinin stem, 
that eventually the virus

00:05:39.289 --> 00:05:41.749
will figure out a way to mutate away 
from that as well.

00:05:41.749 --> 00:05:44.270
An ideal universal influenza vaccine

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:47.909
will not include just one conserved bit 
from the virus,

00:05:47.909 --> 00:05:51.430
but multiple conserved portions from the virus.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:52.430
And if we could do that,

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:55.150
it doesn’t really matter how fast 
we make the vaccine.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.769
Because it would work year after year,

00:05:57.769 --> 00:06:00.786
on multiple different strains of the flu.

00:06:00.786 --> 00:06:02.740
There’s still a bit of research 
left to do:

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:05.000
trials, funding, and more.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:08.557
But we’re closer than you might think.

00:06:08.557 --> 00:06:11.919
FULLER: There are already universal
flu vaccines in clinical trials.

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:15.576
So, we're looking at, you know, years,
a few years down the line.

00:06:15.576 --> 00:06:19.586
KRAMMER: I think 5 to 10 years
is a timeframe that sounds

00:06:19.586 --> 00:06:21.819
at least to me, reasonable.

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:24.949
For most people, the implications 
of a universal flu shot

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:28.385
are bigger than just not having to get 
a vaccine every year.

00:06:28.385 --> 00:06:31.662
It would also make the flu vaccine 
more broadly available.

00:06:31.662 --> 00:06:35.110
KRAMMER: The flu vaccines are
vaccines for rich countries, right?

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:38.090
Low and middle income countries, 
specifically low income countries

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.949
don't have the infrastructure and the capacity
to actually use them.

00:06:41.949 --> 00:06:45.129
A universal flu shot that lasts 
for several years

00:06:45.129 --> 00:06:49.087
could be implemented in 
childhood vaccination programs globally,

00:06:49.087 --> 00:06:54.344
providing better protection for everyone from
current and future outbreaks.

00:06:54.344 --> 00:06:58.160
KRAMMER: We need to kind of put the resources 
into it because otherwise

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:01.048
we won't be prepared for the next 
flu pandemic either.

00:07:01.048 --> 00:07:04.279
FULLER: So, we can do better,
especially with the new technologies

00:07:04.279 --> 00:07:06.738
like mRNA vaccines that have emerged.

00:07:06.738 --> 00:07:10.312
There is a real opportunity to do 
much, much better

00:07:10.312 --> 00:07:12.990
in terms of protecting against flu each year.

